Currently, the default action for an unrecognized arrow type is to ignore it:

https://github.com/mermaid-js/mermaid/blob/2049915c2ad5d1de713548b25d39d9db93d675f5/packages/mermaid/src/dagre-wrapper/edges.js#L564

This PR changes it to instead add an invalid edge marker (could this cause SVG corruption/attacks if we add a string with a `)` in it?)

If we want to the old behavior, maybe something like the following is better?

```ts

const arrowTypesMap = {
  "arrow_cross": "cross",
  "arrow_point": "point",
  "arrow_barb": "barb",
  "arrow_circle": "circle",
  "aggregation": "aggregation",
  "extension": "extension",
  "composition": "composition",
  "dependency": "dependency",
  "lollipop": "lollipop",
} as const;

const addEdgeMarker = (
  svgPath: SVG,
  position: 'start' | 'end',
  arrowType: string,
  url: string,
  id: string,
  diagramType: string
) => {
  if (arrowTypesMap[arrowType] === undefined) {
    // TODO: should we log a debug/warning message?
    return; // unknown arrow type, ignore
  }
  
  const suffix = position === 'start' ? 'Start' : 'End';
  svgPath.attr(`marker-${position}`, `url(${url}#${id}_${diagramType}-${arrowTypesMap[arrowType]}${suffix})`);
};
```

Do we need to test for invalid/malicious arrow types? My gut feeling is that these might produce an invalid SVG file, but I don't enough about SVG to be sure!

```suggestion

  it('should not add invalid markers', () => {
    const arrowTypeStart = 'this is an invalid marker';
    const arrowTypeEnd = ') url(https://my-malicious-site.example)';
    addEdgeMarkers(svgPath, { arrowTypeStart, arrowTypeEnd }, url, id, diagramType);
    expect(svgPath.attr).not.toHaveBeenCalled();
  });
});
```
